window.CHAPTER_DATA = {"number":10,"zh":{"title":"山庄背影","intro":[],"sections":[{"label":"一","paragraphs":["我们这些人，对清代总有一种复杂的情感阻隔。记得很小的时候，历史老师讲到“扬州十日”、“嘉定三屠”时，眼含泪花，这是清代的开始；而讲到“火烧圆明园”、“戊戌变法”时又有泪花了，这是清代的尾声。年迈的老师一哭，孩子们也跟着哭。清代历史，是小学中唯一用眼泪浸润的课程。从小种下的怨恨，很难化解得开。","老人的眼泪和孩子们的眼泪拌和在一起，使这种历史情绪有了一种最世俗的力量。我小学的同学全是汉族，没有满族。因此很容易在课堂里获得一种共同语言，好像汉族理所当然是中国的主宰，你满族为什么要来抢夺呢？抢夺去了能够弄好倒也罢了，偏偏越弄越糟，最后几乎让外国人给瓜分了。于是，在闪闪泪光中，我们懂得了什么是汉奸、什么是卖国贼、什么是民族大义、什么是气节。我们似乎也知道了中国之所以落后于世界列强，关键就在于清代后期的腐败无能，而辛亥革命的启蒙者们重新点燃汉人对这个清朝的仇恨，提出“驱除鞑虏，恢复中华”的口号，又是多么有必要、多么让人解气。清朝终于被推翻了，但至今在很多中国人心里，它仍然是一种冤孽般的存在。","年长以后，我开始对这种情绪产生警惕。因为无数事实证明：在我们中国，许多情绪化的社会评判规范，虽然堂而皇之地传之久远，却包含着极大的不公正。我们缺少人类普遍意义上的价值启蒙，因此这些情绪化的社会评判规范大多是从封建正统观念引申出来的，带有很大盲目性。先是姓氏正统论，刘汉、李唐、赵宋、朱明……在同一姓氏的传代系列中所出现的继承人，哪怕是昏君、懦夫、色鬼、守财奴、精神失常者，都是合法而合理的；而外姓人氏若有觊觎，即便有一千条一万条道理，也站不住脚，真伪、正邪、忠奸全由此划分。由姓氏正统论扩而大之，就是民族正统论。这种观念要比姓氏正统论复杂得多，你看辛亥革命的闯将们与封建主义的姓氏正统论势不两立，却也需要大声宣扬民族正统论，便是例证。","汉族当然非常伟大，没有理由要受到外族的屠杀和欺凌。问题是，不能由此而把汉族等同于中华，把中华历史的正义、光亮、希望全部压在汉族一边。与其他民族一样，汉族也有大量的污浊、昏聩和丑恶，它的统治者曾一再地把整个中国历史推入死胡同。在这种情况下，历史有可能做出超越汉族正统论的选择，而这种选择又未必是倒退。","为此，我要写写承德的避暑山庄。清代的史料成捆成扎，把这些留给历史学家吧，我们，只要轻手轻脚地绕到这个消夏的别墅里去偷看几眼也就够了。"]},{"label":"二","paragraphs":["承德的避暑山庄是清代皇家园林，又称“热河行宫”、“承德离宫”，虽然闻名史册，但久为禁苑，又地处塞外，历来光顾的人不多。我去时，找了山庄背后的一个旅馆住下。那时正是薄暮时分，我独个儿走出住所大门，对着眼前黑黝黝的山岭发呆。查过地图，这山岭便是避暑山庄北部的最后屏障，就像一把罗圈椅的椅背。在这张罗圈椅上，休息过一个疲惫的王朝。","奇怪的是，整个中华版图都已归属了这个王朝，为什么还要把这把休息的罗圈椅放到长城之外呢？清代的帝王们在这张椅子上面南而坐的时候都在想些什么呢？","月亮升起来了，眼前的山壁显得更加巍然怆然。北京的故宫把几个不同的朝代混杂在一起，谁的形象也看不真切；而在这里，远远地、静静地、纯纯地、悄悄地，躲开了中原王气，藏下了一个不羼杂的清代。它实在使我产生了一种巨大的诱惑，从第二天开始，我便一头埋到了山庄里边。","山庄很大，本来觉得北京的颐和园已经大得令人咋舌了，它竟比颐和园还大整整一倍，据说装下八九个北海公园是没有问题的。我想不出国内还有哪个古典园林能望其项背。山庄里面，除了前半部有层层叠叠的宫殿外，是开阔的湖区、平原区和山区。尤其是山区，几乎占了整个山庄的八成，这让游惯了别的园林的人很不习惯。园林是用来休闲的，何况是皇家园林，大多追求方便平适，有的也会堆几座小山装点一下。哪有像这儿的，硬是圈进莽莽苍苍一大片真正的山岭来消遣？这个格局，包含着一种需要我们抬头仰望、低头思索的审美观念和人生观念。","山庄里有很多楹联和石碑，上面的文字大多由皇帝们亲自撰写。他们当然想不到多少年后会有我们这些陌生人闯入他们的私家园林来读这些文字。这些文字是他们写给后辈继承人看的。我踏着青苔和蔓草，辨识和解读着一切能找到的文字，连藏在山间树林中的石碑都不放过。一路走去，终于可以有把握地说：山庄的营造，完全出自一代政治家在精神上的强健。","首先是康熙。他是走了一条艰难而又成功的长途才走进山庄的，到这里来喘口气，应该。","他一生的艰难都是自找的。他的父辈本来已经给他打下了一个很完整的江山，他八岁即位，十四岁亲政，年纪轻轻一个孩子，坐享其成就是了，能在如此辽阔的疆土、如此兴盛的运势前做些什么呢？他稚气未脱的眼睛，竟然疑惑地盯上了两个庞然大物：一个是朝廷中最有权势的辅政大臣鳌拜，一个是自恃当初领清兵入关有功、拥兵自重于南方的吴三桂。平心而论，对于这样与自己的祖辈、父辈都有密切关系的重要政治势力，有几人能下得了决心去动手？但康熙却向他们，也向自己挑战了。他，十六岁上干净利落地除了鳌拜集团，二十岁开始向吴三桂开战，花八年时间的征战取得彻底胜利。","他等于把到手的江山重新打理了一遍，使自己从一个继承者变成了创业者。他成熟了，眼前几乎已经找不到什么对手，但他还是经常骑着马，在中国北方的山林草泽间徘徊，这是他祖辈崛起的所在，他在寻找着自己的生命和事业的依托点。","他每次都要经过长城。长城多年失修，已经破败。对着这堵历代帝王切切关心的城墙，他想了很多。他的祖辈是破长城进来的，没有吴三桂也绝对进得了，那么长城究竟有什么用呢？堂堂一个朝廷，难道就靠这些砖块去保卫？但是如果没有长城，我们的防线又在哪里呢？他思考的结果，可以从一六九一年他的一份上谕中看出个大概。","那年五月，古北口总兵官蔡元向朝廷提出，他所管辖的那一带长城“倾塌甚多，请行修筑”，康熙竟然不同意，他的上谕是：","秦筑长城以来，汉、唐、宋亦常修理，其时岂无边患？明末我太祖统大兵长驱直入，诸路瓦解，皆莫能当。可见守国之道，唯在修德安民。民心悦则邦本得，而边境自固，所谓“众志成城”者是也。如古北、喜峰口一带，朕皆巡阅，概多损坏，今欲修之，兴工劳役，岂能无害百姓？且长城延袤数千里，养兵几何方能分守？","说得实在是很有道理。","康熙希望能筑起一座无形的长城。对此，他有硬的一手和软的一手。硬的一手是在长城外设立“木兰围场”，每年秋天，由皇帝亲自率领王公大臣、各级官兵一万余人去进行大规模的“围猎”，实际上是一种声势浩大的军事演习，这既可以使王公大臣们保持住勇猛、强悍的人生风范，又可顺便对北方边境起一个威慑作用。“木兰围场”既然设在长城之外的边远地带，离北京就很有一点距离，如此众多的朝廷要员前去秋猎，当然要建造一些大大小小的行宫，而热河行宫就是其中最大的一座。","软的一手是与北方边疆的各少数民族建立起一种常来常往的友好关系，他们的首领不必长途进京也能有与清廷交谊的场所。而且还为他们准备下各自的宗教场所，这也就需要有热河行宫和它周围的寺庙群了。","总之，软硬两手最后都汇集到这一座行宫、这一个山庄里来了，说是避暑，说是休息，意义却又远远不止于此。把复杂的政治目的转化为一片幽静闲适的园林、一圈香火缭绕的寺庙，这不能不说是康熙的大本事。","康熙几乎每年立秋之后都要到“木兰围场”参加一次为期二十天的秋猎，一生共参加了四十八次。每次围猎，情景都极为壮观。先由康熙选定逐年轮换的狩猎区域，然后就搭建一百七十多座大帐篷为“内城”、二百五十多座大帐篷为“外城”，城外再设警卫。第二天拂晓，八旗官兵在皇帝的统一督导下集结围拢。在上万官兵的齐声呐喊下，康熙一马当先，引弓射猎，每有所中便引来一片欢呼。然后，扈从大臣和各级将士也紧随康熙射猎。","康熙身强力壮，骑术高明，围猎时智勇双全，弓箭上的功夫更让王公大臣由衷惊服，因而他本人的猎获就很多。","晚上，营地上篝火处处，肉香飘荡，人笑马嘶，而康熙还必须回到帐篷里批阅每天疾驰送来的奏章文书。","康熙一生打过许多著名的仗，但在晚年，他最得意的还是自己打猎的成绩，因为这纯粹是他个人生命力的验证。一七一九年康熙自“木兰围场”行猎后返回避暑山庄时，曾兴致勃勃地告谕御前侍卫：","朕自幼至今，凡用鸟枪弓矢获虎一百三十五，熊二十，豹二十五，猞猁狲十，麋十四，狼九十六，野猪一百三十二，哨获之鹿数百，其余围场内随便射获诸兽不胜记矣。朕曾于一日内射兔三百一十八，若庸常人，毕世亦不能得此一日之数也。","这笔流水账，他说得很得意，我们读得也很高兴。身体的强健和精神的强健是连在一起的，须知中国历史上多的是病恹恹的皇帝，他们即便再“内秀”，却何以面对如此庞大的国家？","由于强健，他有足够的精力处理复杂的西藏事务和蒙古事务，解决治理黄河、淮河和疏通漕运等大问题，而且大多很有成效，功泽后世。由于强健，他还愿意勤奋地学习，结果不仅武功一流，“内秀”也十分了得，成为中国历代皇帝中特别有学问，也特别重视学问的一位。","谁能想得到呢，这位清朝帝王竟然比明代历朝皇帝更热爱汉族传统文化。大凡经、史、子、集、诗、书、音律，他都下过一番工夫，其中对朱熹哲学钻研最深。他亲自批点《资治通鉴纲目大全》，还下令访求遗散在民间的善本珍籍加以整理，大规模组织人力编辑出版了卷帙浩繁的《古今图书集成》和字典辞书，文化气魄铺天盖地。直到今天，我们研究中国古代文化还离不开那些重要的工具书。在他倡导的文化气氛下，涌现了一大批优秀的文史专家。在这一点上，很少有哪个年代能与康熙朝相比肩。","以上讲的还只是我们所说的“国学”，可能更让现代读者惊异的是他的“西学”。因为即使到了现代，在我们印象中，国学和西学虽然可以沟通，但在同一个人身上深谙两边的毕竟不多。然而早在三百年前，康熙皇帝竟然在北京故宫和承德避暑山庄认真研究了欧几里得几何学，经常演算习题，又学习了法国数学家巴蒂的《实用和理论几何学》，并比较它与欧几里得几何学的差别。他的老师是当时来中国的一批西方传教士，但后来他的演算比传教士还快。以数学为基础，康熙又进而学习了西方的天文、历法、物理、医学，与中国原有的这方面知识比较，取长补短。在自然科学问题上，中国官僚和外国传教士经常发生矛盾，康熙从不袒护中国官僚，也不主观臆断，而是靠自己认真学习，几乎每次都作出了公正的裁断。","这一切，居然与他所醉心的“国学”互不排斥，居然与他一天射猎三百一十八只野兔互不排斥，居然与他一连串重大的政治行为、军事行为、经济行为互不排斥！","我并不认为康熙给中国带来了根本性的希望，他的政权也做过不少坏事，如臭名昭著的文字狱之类。我想说的只是，在中国历代帝王中，这位少数民族出身的帝王具有异乎寻常的生命力，他的人格比较健全。","有时，个人的生命力和人格会给历史留下重重的印记。与他相比，明代的许多皇帝都活得太不像样了，鲁迅说他们是“无赖儿郎”，的确有点像。尤其让人生气的是明代万历皇帝（神宗）朱翊钧，在位四十八年，亲政三十八年，竟有二十五年时间躲在深宫之内不见外人的面，完全不理国事，连内阁首辅也见不到他，不知在干什么。他聚敛的金银如山似海，但当辽东起事、朝廷束手无策时问他要钱，他死也不肯拿出来，最后拿出一个无济于事的小零头，竟然都是因窖藏太久变黑发霉、腐蚀得不能见天日的银子！这是一个失去了人格支撑的心理变态者，但他又集权于一身，明朝怎能不垮？他死后还有后代继位，但明朝已在他的手里败定了。康熙与他正相反，把生命从深宫里释放出来，在旷野、猎场和各个知识领域挥洒，避暑山庄就是他这种生命方式的一个重要吐纳点。"]},{"label":"三","paragraphs":["康熙与晚明帝王的对比，避暑山庄与万历深宫的对比，当时的汉族知识分子当然也感受到了，心情比较复杂。","开始，大多数汉族知识分子都坚持抗清复明，甚至在赳赳武夫们纷纷掉头转向之后，一群柔弱的文人还宁死不屈。文人中也有一些著名的变节者，但他们往往也承受着深刻的心理矛盾和精神痛苦。","我想这便是文化的力量。一切军事争逐都是浮面的，而事情到了要摇撼某个文化生态系统的时候才会真正变得严重起来。","一个民族、一个国家、一个人种，其最终意义不是军事的、地域的、政治的，而是文化的。当时江南地区好几次重大的抗清事件，都起于“削发”之事，即汉人历来束发而清人强令削发，甚至到了“留头不留发，留发不留头”的地步。头发的样式看来事小，却关及文化生态。结果，是否“毁我衣冠”的问题成了“夷夏抗争”的最高爆发点。","这中间，最能把事情与整个文化系统联系起来的是文化人，最懂得文明和野蛮的差别，并把“鞑虏”与野蛮连在一起的也是文化人。老百姓的头发终于被削掉了，而不少文人还在拼死坚持。著名大学者刘宗周住在杭州，自清兵进杭州后便绝食，二十天后死亡；他的门生、另一位著名大学者黄宗羲投身于武装抗清行列，失败后回余姚家乡事母、著述；又一位著名大学者顾炎武，武装抗清失败后便开始流浪，谁也找不着他，最后终老陕西……这些宗师如此强硬，他们的门生和崇拜者们当然也多有追随。","但是，事情到了康熙那儿却发生了一些微妙的变化。文人们依然像朱耷笔下的秃鹰，以“天地为之一寒”的冷眼看着朝廷，而朝廷却奇怪地流泻出一种压抑不住的对汉文化的热忱。开始大家以为是一种笼络人心的策略，但从康熙身上看，好像不完全是。","他在讨伐吴三桂的战争还没有结束的时候，就迫不及待地下令各级官员以“崇儒重道”为目的，向朝廷推荐“学问兼优、文辞卓越”的士子，由他亲自主考录用，称做“博学鸿词科”。","这次被保荐、征召的共一百四十三人，后来录取了五人。其中有傅山、李颙等人被推荐了却宁死不应考。傅山被人推荐后又被强抬进北京，他见到“大清门”三字便滚倒在地，两泪直流。如此行动举止，康熙不仅不怪罪，反而免他考试，任命他为“中书舍人”。他回乡后不准别人以“中书舍人”称他，但这个时候说他对康熙本人还有多大仇恨，大概谈不上了。","李颙也是如此，受到推荐后称病拒考，被人抬到省城后竟以绝食相抗，众人只得作罢。这事发生在康熙十七年，康熙本人二十五岁。没想到二十五年后，年过半百的康熙西巡时还记得这位强硬的学人，要召见他；李颙没有应召，但心里毕竟已经很过意不去了，派儿子李慎言做代表应召，并送自己的两部著作《四书反身录》和《二曲集》给康熙。这件事带有一定的象征性，表示最有抵触的汉族知识分子也开始与康熙和解了。","与李颙相比，黄宗羲是大人物了。康熙对黄宗羲更是礼仪有加，多次请黄宗羲出山未能如愿，便命令当地巡抚到黄宗羲家里，把黄宗羲写的书认真抄来，送入宫内以供自己拜读。这一来，黄宗羲也不能不有所感动。与李颙一样，自己出面终究不便，由儿子代理，黄宗羲让自己的儿子黄百家进入皇家修史部门，帮助完成康熙交下的修《明史》的任务。你看，即便是原先与清廷不共戴天的黄宗羲、李颙他们，也觉得儿子一辈可以在康熙手下好生过日子了。这不是变节，也不是妥协，而是一种文化生态意义上的开始认同。既然康熙对汉文化认同得那么诚恳，汉族文人为什么就完全不能与他认同呢？","黄宗羲不是让儿子参加康熙下令编写的《明史》吗？编《明史》这事给汉族知识界震动不小。康熙任命了大历史学家徐元文、万斯同、张玉书、王鸿绪等负责此事，要他们根据《明实录》如实编写，说“他书或以文章见长，独修史宜直书实事”。他还多次要大家仔细研究明代晚期破败的教训，引以为戒。汉族知识界要反清复明，而清廷君主竟然亲自领导着汉族的历史学家在冷静研究明代了。这种研究又高于反清复明者的思考水平，那么，对峙也就不能不渐渐化解了。《明史》后来成为整个二十四史中写得较好的一部，这是直到今天还要承认的事实。","当然，也还余留着几个坚持不肯认同的文人。例如，康熙时代浙江有个叫吕留良的学者，在著书和讲学中还一再强调孔子思想的精义是“尊王攘夷”。这个提法，在他死后被湖南一个叫曾静的落第书生看到了，很是激动，赶到浙江找到吕留良的儿子和学生几人，筹划反清。","这时康熙也早已过世，已是雍正年间，这群文人手下无一兵一卒，能干成什么事呢？他们打听到川陕总督岳钟琪是岳飞的后代，想来肯定能继承岳飞遗志来抗击外夷，就派人带给他一封策反的信，眼巴巴地请他起事。","这事说起来已经有点近乎笑话。岳飞抗金到那时已隔着整整一个元朝、整整一个明朝，清朝也已过了八九十年，算到岳钟琪身上都是多少代的事啦，居然还想着让他凭着一个“岳”字拍案而起，中国书生的昏愚和天真就在这里。","岳钟琪是清朝大官，做梦也没有想到过要反清，接信后虚假地应付了一下，却理所当然地报告了雍正皇帝。雍正下令逮捕了这个谋反集团，又亲自阅读了书信、著作，觉得其中有好些观点需要自己写文章来与汉族知识分子辩论。他认为有过康熙一代，已有足够的事实证明清代统治者并不差，可为什么还有人要对抗清廷？于是这位皇帝亲自编了一部《大义觉迷录》颁发各地，而且特免肇事者曾静等人的死罪，让他们专到江浙一带去宣讲。","雍正的《大义觉迷录》写得颇为诚恳。他的大意是：不错，我们是夷人，我们是“外国”人，但这是籍贯而已，天命要我们来抚育中原生民，被抚育者为什么还要把华、夷分开来看？你们所尊重的舜是东夷之人、文王是西夷之人，这难道有损于他们的圣德吗？吕留良这样著书立说的人，将前朝康熙皇帝的文治武功、赫赫盛德都加以隐匿和诬蔑，实在是不顾民生国运只泄私愤了。外族入主中原，可能反而勇于为善，如果著书立说的人只认为生在中原的君主不必修德行仁也可享有名分，而外族君主即便励精图治也得不到褒扬，外族君主为善之心也会因之而懈怠，受苦的不还是中原百姓吗？","雍正的这番话带着明显的委屈情绪，而且是给父亲康熙打抱不平，也真有一些动人的地方。但他的整体思维显然比不上康熙，口口声声说自己是“外国”人、“夷人”，在一些前提性的概念上把事情搞复杂了。他的儿子乾隆看出了这个毛病，即位后把《大义觉迷录》全部收回，列为禁书，杀了被雍正赦免的曾静等人，开始大兴文字狱。","除了华、夷之分的敏感点外，其他地方雍正倒是比较宽容、有度量，听得进忠臣贤士们的尖锐意见和建议，因此在执政的前期，做了不少好事，国运可称昌盛。这样一来，即便存有异念的少数汉族知识分子也不敢有什么想头，到后来也真没有什么想头了。其实本来这样的人已不可多觅，雍正和乾隆都把文章做过了头。真正第一流的大学者，在乾隆时代已经不想做反清复明的事情。","乾隆靠着人才济济的智力优势，靠着康熙、雍正给他奠定的丰厚基业，也靠着他本人的韬略雄才，做起了中国历史上福气最好的大皇帝。承德避暑山庄，他来得最多，总共逗留的时间很长，因此他的踪迹更是随处可见。乾隆也经常参加“木兰秋猎”，亲自射获的猎物也极为可观，但他的主要心思却放在边疆征战上，避暑山庄和周围的外八庙内记载这种征战成果的碑文极多。","这种征战与汉族的利益没有冲突，反而弘扬了中国的国威，连汉族知识界也引以为荣，甚至可以把乾隆看成是华夏圣君了。但我细看碑文之后却产生一个强烈的感觉：有的仗迫不得已，打打也可以，但多数战争的必要性深可怀疑——需要打得这么大吗？需要反复那么多次吗？需要杀得如此残酷吗？","好大喜功的乾隆把他的所谓“十全武功”雕刻在避暑山庄里乐滋滋地自我品尝，这使山庄回荡出一些燥热而又不祥的气氛。在满、汉文化对峙基本上结束之后，这里洋溢着的是中华帝国的自得情绪。","一七九三年九月十四日，一个英国使团来到避暑山庄，乾隆以盛宴欢迎，还在山庄的万树园内以大型歌舞和焰火晚会招待，避暑山庄一片热闹。英方的目的是希望乾隆同意他们派使臣常驻北京，在北京设立洋行，希望中国开放贸易口岸，在广州附近拨一些地方让英商居住，又希望英国货物在广州至澳门的内河流通时能获免税和减税的优惠。本来，这是可以谈判的事，但对于居住在避暑山庄、一生喜欢用武力炫耀华夏威仪的乾隆来说，却不存在任何谈判的可能。","他给英国国王写了信，信的标题是“赐英吉利国王敕书”。信内对一切要求全部拒绝，说：“天朝尺土俱归版籍，疆址森然，即岛屿沙洲，亦必划界分疆，各有专属”，“从无外人等在北京城开设货行之事”，“此与天朝体制不合，断不可行”。至今有人认为这几句话充满了爱国主义的凛然大义，与以后清廷签订的卖国条约不可同日而语。对此我实在不敢苟同。","本来康熙早在一六八四年就已开放海禁，在广东、福建、浙江、江苏分设四个海关欢迎外商来贸易。过了七十多年，乾隆反而关闭其他海关只许外商在广州贸易。外商在广州也有许多可笑的限制，例如，不准学说中国话、买中国书，不许坐轿，更不许把妇女带来，等等。我们闭目就能想象清廷对外国人的这些限制是出于何种心理规定出来的。","康熙向传教士学西方自然科学，关系不错，而乾隆却把天主教给禁了。","乾隆在避暑山庄训斥外国帝王的朗声言辞，在历史老人听来，不太顺耳了。这座园林已掺杂进某种凶兆。"]},{"label":"四","paragraphs":["我在山庄松云峡乾隆诗碑的西侧，读到了他儿子嘉庆写的一首诗。嘉庆即位后经过这里，看到父亲那些得意扬扬的诗作后不禁长叹一声：父亲的诗真是深奥，而我这个做儿子的却实在觉得肩上的担子太重了！（“瞻题蕴精奥，守位重仔肩。”）","嘉庆一生都在面对内忧外患，最后不明不白地死在避暑山庄。","道光皇帝继嘉庆之位时已近四十岁，没有什么才能，只知艰苦朴素，穿的裤子还打过补丁。这对一国元首来说可不是什么佳话。朝中大臣竞相模仿，穿了破旧衣服上朝，一眼看去，这个朝廷已经没有多少气数了。","父亲死在避暑山庄，畏怯的道光也就不愿意去那里了，让它空关了几十年。他有时想想也该像祖宗一样去打一次猎，打听能不能不经过避暑山庄就可以到“木兰围场”，回答说没有别的道路，他也就不去打猎了。像他这么个可怜巴巴的皇帝，似乎本来就与山庄和打猎没有缘分；鸦片战争已经爆发，他忧愁的目光只能一直注视着南方。","避暑山庄一直关到一八六○年九月，突然接到命令，咸丰皇帝要来，赶快打扫。咸丰这次来时带的银两特别多，原来是来逃难的，英法联军正威胁着北京。咸丰这一来就不走了，东走走西看看，庆幸祖辈留下这么个好地方让他躲避。他在这里又批准了好几份丧权辱国的条约，但签约后还是不走，直到一八六一年七月十七日死在这儿，差不多住了近一年。","咸丰一死，避暑山庄热闹了好些天，各种政治势力围着遗体进行着明明暗暗的较量。一场被历史学家称为“辛酉政变”的行动方案在山庄的几间屋子里制订。然后，咸丰的灵柩向北京起运了，刚继位的小皇帝也出发了，浩浩荡荡。避暑山庄的大门，又一次紧紧地关住了。而在这支浩浩荡荡的队伍中间，很快站出来一个二十七岁的青年女子，她将统治中国数十年。","她就是慈禧，离开了山庄后再也没有回来。不久她又下了一道命令，说热河避暑山庄已经几十年不用，殿亭各宫多已倾圮，只是咸丰皇帝去时稍稍修治了一下，现在咸丰已逝，众人已走，“所有热河一切工程，着即停止”。","这个命令，与康熙不修长城的谕旨前后辉映。康熙的“长城”也终于倾塌了，荒草凄迷，暮鸦回翔，旧墙斑驳，霉苔处处，而大门却紧紧地关着。","关住了那些宫殿房舍倒也罢了，还关住了那么些苍郁的山、那么些晶亮的水。在康熙看来，这儿就是他心目中的清王朝，但清王朝把它丢弃了。被丢弃了的它可怜，丢弃了它的清王朝更可怜，连一把罗圈椅也坐不到了，恓恓惶惶，丧魂落魄。","后来慈禧在北京重修了一个颐和园，与避暑山庄“对峙”。塞外朔北的园林不会再有对峙的能力和兴趣，它似乎已属于另外一个时代。热河的雄风早已吹散，清朝从此阴气重重、劣迹斑斑。","当新的一个世纪来到的时候，一大群汉族知识分子向这个政权发出了毁灭性声讨。避暑山庄，在这个时候是一个邪恶的象征，老老实实躲在远处，尽量不要叫人发现。"]},{"label":"五","paragraphs":["清王朝灭亡后，社会震荡，世事忙乱。直到一九二七年六月二日，大学者王国维先生在颐和园投水而死，才让全国的有心人肃然沉思。","王国维先生的死因众说纷纭，我们且不管它，只知道这位汉族文化大师拖着清代的一条辫子，自尽在清代的皇家园林里，遗嘱为“五十之年，只欠一死；经此世变，义无再辱”。","他不会不知道明末清初为汉族人是束发还是留辫之争曾发生过惊人的血案，他不会不知道刘宗周、黄宗羲、顾炎武这些大学者的慷慨行迹，他更不会不知道按照世界历史的进程，社会巨变乃属必然。但是，他还是死了。","我赞成陈寅恪先生的说法，王国维先生并不是死于政治斗争、人事纠葛，而是死于一种文化：","凡一种文化值衰落之时，为此文化所化之人，必感苦痛，其表现此文化之程量愈宏，则其所受之苦痛亦愈甚；迨既达极深之度，殆非出于自杀无以求一己之心安而义尽也。","（《王观堂先生挽词并序》）","王国维先生实在无法把文化与清廷分割开来。在他的书架里，《古今图书集成》、《康熙字典》、《四库全书》、《红楼梦》、《桃花扇》、《长生殿》、乾嘉学派、纳兰性德都历历在目，每一本、每一页都无法分割。在他看来，在他身边陨灭的，不仅仅是一个政治意义上，而且更是一个文化意义上的古典时代。","他，只想留在古典时代。","我们记得，在康熙手下，汉族高层知识分子经过剧烈的心理挣扎已开始与朝廷建立文化认同，没有想到的是，当康熙的事业破败之后，文化认同还未消散。为此，宏才博学的王国维先生要以生命来祭奠它。他没有从心理挣扎中找到希望，死得可惜又死得必然。","知识分子总是不同寻常，他们总要在政治、军事的折腾之后表现出长久的文化韧性。文化变成了他们的生命，只有靠生命来拥抱文化了，别无他途。明末以后是这样，清末以后也是这样。","文化的极度脆弱和极度强大，都在王国维先生纵身投水的扑通声中呈现无遗。","王国维先生到颐和园这也还是第一次，是从一个同事处借了五元钱才去的。颐和园门票六角，死后口袋中尚余四元四角。他去不了承德，也推不开山庄紧闭的大门。","今天，我面对着避暑山庄的清澈湖水，却不能不想起王国维先生的面容和身影。我轻轻地叹息一声：一个风云数百年的朝代，总是以一群强者英武的雄姿开头，而打下最后一个句点的，却常常是一些文质彬彬的凄怨灵魂。","秋雨注：这篇文章发表于一九九三年，后来被中国评论界看成是全部“清宫电视剧”的肇始之文。“清宫电视剧”拍得不错，但整体历史观念与我有很大差别。我对清代宫廷的看法，可参见本书另一篇《宁古塔》。"]}]},"en":{"title":"The Villa from Behind","intro":[],"sections":[{"label":"I","paragraphs":["PEOPLE LIKE US have a complicated emotional reaction regarding the Qing Dynasty. As a child, I remember my history teacher’s eyes would begin to fill with tears as he spoke of the “Ten Days in Yangzhou” or “The Three Jiading Massacres,” because they marked the start of the Qing Dynasty. He would also weep when it came time to talk about the “Burning of the Old Summer Palace” or “The Hundred Days’ Reform” in the dying days of the Qing. Seeing our elderly teacher crying, naturally us kids began sobbing too. These were the only tears I shed during the whole of elementary school. A grudge put in place this early on is difficult to get rid of.","When the tears of old people and children mingle, a historical atmosphere is created with a most tangible power. My classmates in elementary school were all Han Chinese, with not a single Manchu amongst them. It was therefore easy to fall into a common vocabulary in the classroom, as if it were completely natural that the Han should be the rulers of China, and why would you Manchus come try to snatch it away from us? If you’d grabbed it and made it better, that would have been one thing, but you made the country worse and worse, until by the end it was practically being carved up by foreigners. With tears falling freely, we learned what it was to be a traitor, what it meant to sell off your country to the highest bidder, what nationalism was, and the definition of integrity. We seemed to understand that the reason China had fallen behind and was no longer a world power, was all because of the Qing Dynasty, until the instigators of the Xinhai Rebellion lit the fuse of the Han people’s anger against the regime, raising the slogan “Expel the Manchus and Restore China”—a necessary, cathartic movement. And so the Qing Dynasty was overturned, but even till this day, it lurks in the hearts of many Chinese people, like a form of retribution.","When I grew up, I became wary of this mindset. Countless events have shown that in China, many societal judgments are emotionally based, and while they may have been imposed on generation after generation, they contain vast injustices within them. We lack the enlightened values of common humanity. These emotionally charged values are often derived from the orthodoxies of feudal times, and so have huge blind spots. There’s the idea of continuity by surname—Liu in the Han Dynasty, Li in the Tang, Zhao in the Song, Zhu in the Ming—and as long as each successor has the right surname, it doesn’t matter if they’re an incompetent ruler, a coward, a pervert, a miser, or someone with mental difficulties; their right to take over is seen as preordained, and if someone with an outsider’s surname were to try to muscle in, a thousand and one reasons could be found why they didn’t have a leg to stand on, their name being the sole determiner of truth or falsehood, goodness or evil, loyalty or treachery. Expanding this mindset, we arrive at tribalism—a way of thinking with a bit more complexity than the former, as we see in the rupture during the Xinhai Revolution, when the generals and the feudalists clashed over surname orthodoxy, but were forced to loudly proclaim that they ultimately supported this chauvinism.","The Han are, of course, a very noble people, and have never deserved the slaughter or humiliation inflicted by outsiders. But we can’t therefore equate the Han tribe with Chinese culture, and credit them with all the righteousness, brilliance and hope of Chinese history. As with all other communities, the Han had their share of corruption, muddled thinking, and ugly behavior, and at one point their proponents were trying to shove the whole of Chinese history down a dead end. In these circumstances, history made the choice of transcending Han orthodoxy, which does not necessarily represent a retreat.","And so, I’d like to write a little about a mountain house in Chengde. The history of the Qing Dynasty is tangled and knotted, and I will leave it to the historians to unravel. Let us instead move lightly over to this summer villa, where a few quick glances will satisfy us."]},{"label":"II","paragraphs":["THE CHENGDE MOUNTAIN RESORT BELONGED to the Qing Imperial family, and was also known as the Hot River Palace and the Chengde Distant Residence. Although this is a famous historical site, it was a forbidden building for a long time, as well as being located beyond the Great Wall, so there have never been very many visitors here. After arriving, I checked into a hotel behind the villa. It was twilight when I stepped out alone through the main lobby, and stood gazing at the dark bulk of the mountains before me. Looking at the map, this range was the final barrier to the north of the resort, like the back of a round-framed chair. An exhausted dynasty rested on this chair.","The strange thing was, with the entirety of China at the disposal of the dynasty, why locate this place of rest beyond the Great Wall? What did the Qing Emperors ponder as they sat in this seat, facing south?","As the moon rose, the slopes before me grew even more majestic and melancholy. Beijing’s Forbidden Palace muddles several different dynasties together so their outlines are blurred; in this place, so distant, so still, so pure, so quiet, the authority of the Central Plains was concealed, and the Qing Dynasty reposed here unmixed with other influences. All of this exerted an enormous pull on me, and I resolved that from the next day, I would devote my time to the villa.","The palace was huge. The size of Beijing’s Summer Palace had already left me stunned, and this place was actually twice as big. You could probably fit eight or nine Beihai Parks in here. I can’t think of a single ancient garden anywhere in China that comes close to this. After the various palaces gathered at the front of the property, the premises opened out into lakes, plains and hills. The hills, in particular, seemed to occupy eight-tenths of the land, an unusual proportion for those of us used to the layout of other parks. Landscaped gardens are presumably intended for relaxation, particularly imperial ones, and mostly aim for convenience and ease. Some scatter a few hillocks around for ornamentation, but nothing like this, a vast, desolate stretch of actual mountain range—what kind of recreation would this provide? Such a vista commands us to raise our heads and behold it in its entirety, and then look back down and contemplate its aesthetics and our outlook on life.","Also within the grounds were many couplets on stone tablets, many of them inscribed personally by the various Emperors. Of course, they never imagined that many decades later, strangers like us would be barging into their private gardens and reading these words, written with their successors and descendants in mind. Treading across moss and thick grass, I pieced together and read what text I could find, even seeking out the stone tablets deep within the hilly woods. As I walked, I could finally pronounce with confidence: the construction of this mountain villa was the result of a generation of politicians’ psychological strength.","The first of these was Emperor Kangxi. He walked a long, arduous road before succeeding. He walked into the villa to draw breath—as well he should.","All the challenges of his life were self-imposed. His father’s generation had already presented him with a most complete set of mountains and rivers. He ascended the throne at the age of eight, and began governing at fourteen. Although just a child, he had no need to do anything but stay put. What more could he achieve, already possessing such sweeping territories, such enormous good fortune? Yet his barely mature eyes were fixed mistrustfully on two powerful figures: the most powerful of his regents, Oboi, and the arrogant general Wu Sangui, who’d initially led the Qing troops into China, and held great authority over his forces. In fairness, how many people would be determined enough to strike against important political figures with such close relationships to one’s own forebears? But Kangxi did battle with them, and also with himself. At sixteen, he felled the entire Oboi clan in one clean blow. At twenty, he launched an attack on Wu Sangui, spending eight years on this conflict before securing a decisive victory.","This was equivalent to him reevaluating the lands under his command, turning himself from an inheritor to a creator. He was an adult now, and could see no opponents before him, yet still frequently rode his horse through the forests and grasslands of the north, from where his ancestors rose up, searching for a resting point for his own life and endeavors.","On every trip, he passed by the Great Wall, now neglected for many years and looking distinctly dilapidated. He thought a great deal about this structure that had been so important to generations of Emperors. His ancestors had broken through the Wall to enter this country, and would have managed to do so even without the leadership of Wu Sangui. So what use was this Wall, then? Could such a mighty dynasty really need a pile of bricks to protect it? Yet without the Wall, where would our line of defense be? The results of his contemplation can be seen in an imperial edict dating from 1691.","That May, the Commander-in-Chief of the forces at Gubeikou, Cai Yuan, pointed out to the court that the stretch of wall under his charge was “riddled with collapsed sections” and requested that “repair work be carried out.” Kangxi, remarkably, disagreed, and his edict was:","Ever since the Qin built the Wall, the Han, Tang and Song have often maintained it, but where are the threats to our borders now? At the end of the Ming, my ancestors led an army and broke through, clearing all roads before them, overcoming all obstacles. We can see, then, that in order to protect the kingdom, all that requires maintenance is our virtue and the peace of the people. If the people are happy, the land will be secure. The only way to reinforce our borders is by heeding the saying “the will of the people creates the wall.” Places such as Gubei and Xifengkou we have patrolled and inspected, and while there is much damage, if we wish to repair this, it will inevitably require forced labor, which would surely be detrimental to the people. In any case, the Great Wall stretches for thousands of li—how many soldiers would we require to properly defend it?","All of which is perfectly reasonable.","Kangxi wished to construct an intangible Great Wall. To this end, he was harsh with one hand and gentle with the other. The hard aspect established the “Mulan Hunting Reserve” beyond the Great Wall, and personally led the aristocracy and ministers, not to mention more than ten thousand soldiers of all ranks, on a large scale “enclosed hunt” every fall. In practice, this was a large-scale military training exercise, enabling the aristocrats and government officials to maintain a intrepid, valiant mode of being, while incidentally carrying out a little saber-rattling toward the northern border. As the Mulan Hunting Reserve was in the area outside the Great Wall, it was some little distance from Beijing, and with so many important courtiers involved in the fall hunt, naturally some temporary palaces were required to accommodate them. The Hot River Palace was the largest of these.","The softer aim was to become good friends with the various minority tribes along the northern border regions, setting up frequent exchanges with them, creating sites where the leaders of these communities could meet Qing officials without making the long journey to the capital. He also made sure they each had their own places of worship, which was another reason for the existence of the Hot Water Palace and its surrounding temples.","In the end, both these tactics converged on a single palace, this mountain resort. While its stated purpose was to escape the heat of summer or to provide a place of rest, the significance of this building went far beyond that. It’s hard to deny that Kangxi’s great achievement was to transform the complexities of political motives into a peaceful, serene garden, and temples filled with tendrils of incense smoke.","Almost every year, as soon as fall arrived, Kangxi was obliged to head to Mulan Hunting Reserve to take part in a twenty-day autumn hunt—over his lifetime, he participated in forty-eight of these. The proceedings would always be magnificent. First, Kangxi would select the hunting zone—it changed every year—and then an “inner city” of 170 tents would be raised, with 250 around these as the “outer city,” and a ring of guardsmen beyond that. At dawn the next morning, armies of the Eight Banners would gather under the command of the Emperor, more than ten thousand of them cheering loudly as they rode forth, with Kangxi at their head, bows at the ready, hurrahs greeting every fresh kill. Later, Emperor would also lead a retinue of minsters and generals on a hunt.","Kangxi was strong and vigorous, with excellent horseback skills. He had both the courage and intelligence needed in a formidable huntsman. His prowess with the bow left the watching aristocrats and officials in awe, and his tally of kills at each expedition was always spectacular.","At night, campfires would be dotted across the land, the aroma of roasting meat rising from them amid laughter and horses neighing, while Kangxi was obliged to retire to his tent, where he would read through the court reports delivered each day.","Emperor Kangxi fought many famous battles over the course of his life, but in his later years, he remained proudest of his achievements while hunting, because they alone were a proof of his vitality. In 1719, Kangxi returned from a Mulan hunt and excitedly told his bodyguard:","Since I was a child, with my musket or bow and arrow, I’ve killed 135 tigers, 20 bears, 25 leopards, 10 lynxes, 14 river deer, 96 wolves, and 132 wild boar. My men have bagged several hundred deer, and countless other beasts that we shot around the reserve. In one day, I shot 318 rabbits. That’s more than normal people kill in their entire lifetime.","He seems terribly proud of this account, and it brings us joy to read it. Physical strength and vigor of the spirit go together—yet you should know that Chinese history has been populated with feeble, sickly Emperors who, no matter how “quietly intelligent” they might have been, would surely have found it tricky to govern such a vast country.","Kangxi’s good constitution gave him all the energy he needed to deal with the fiendish situations in Tibet and Mongolia, to bring the Yellow River and Huai River under control, to solve the problem of transporting goods by the waterways—most of which he handled very successfully, with results that continued to benefit future generations. He also had the energy to study constantly, resulting in a “quiet intelligence” to add to his military prowess. Of all the Chinese emperors, he was the most learned, and also the one who valued learning the most.","Who could have imagined that this Qing Dynasty Emperor would turn out to be even more passionate about traditional Han culture than any of the Ming? Whether it was scriptures, histories, the writings of Confucius, anthologies, poems, letters or tomes, he’d put effort into studying them, particularly in analyzing the philosophy of Zhu Xi. He personally annotated A Mirror for the Wise, and sent out orders for rare books in possession of the common people to be acquired and restored. He set in motion the massive task of compiling and publishing the enormous Imperial Encyclopedia and a dictionary, infusing the nation with a literary atmosphere. To this day, when researching ancient Chinese culture, we’re still very dependent on these reference books. With his support, there was a flourishing of literature and history scholarship, and very few other eras could compete with the renaissance that Kangxi presided over.","The above only covers what we’ve already talked about, “national studies,” but there was also a subject startling even to modern readers—”Western studies.” Our impression today is that Chinese and Western studies have some common ground, but few people really have a deep knowledge of both. Yet three hundred years ago, in both Beijing’s Forbidden City and the Chengde Villa, Kangxi made a serious study of Euclid’s theories of geometry, often working his way through practice problems, and also analyzing the French mathematician Pardies’ Practical and Theoretical Geometry, comparing his methods to those of Euclid. His teachers were missionaries who’d come to China from the West, but he was soon able to perform calculations faster than they did. With mathematics as his foundation, Kangxi began studying Western ideas of astronomy, calendar science, physics, and medicine, expanding China’s base of knowledge in these areas, gaining new concepts and patching up gaps. When it came to the natural sciences, the Chinese bureaucrats frequently clashed with the missionaries, and on these occasions Kangxi neither defended the Chinese side nor made assumptions about who might be right, but diligently worked through the problem for himself, and almost every time made a fair judgment","All of this was, remarkably, compatible with the “national studies” that intoxicated him, the hunting habit that saw him shoot 318 wild rabbits in a single day, and the important political, military, and economic projects he undertook.","I don’t believe that Kangxi brought a fundamental new hope to China—his regime was responsible for plenty of evil deeds, such as the notorious Literary Inquisition. All I’m saying is that in the history of Chinese emperors, this one from a minority tribe possessed extraordinary vitality, and his character was better formed than most.","There are times when a person’s life force and character can leave a deep imprint on history. Compared to Kangxi, many of the Ming Emperors were very badly behaved, and Lu Xun was not wrong to describe them as “a pack of feckless chaps.” The one who makes me most angry is the Wanli Emperor, (Shenzong) Zhu Yijun. He held power for forty-eight years, the first ten under a regent. For twenty-five of these, he buried himself deep in the palace, refusing to see any outsiders and neglecting affairs of state completely, so that even the ministers of his cabinet were unable to meet him. I don’t know what he was doing all that time. He accumulated mountains of wealth, but when there were problems on the Liaodong Peninsula and the court, at a loss for funds, begged him for money, he refused to hand any over, finally releasing only a pittance that did no good for the situation at hand—silver pieces covered in mold, having been kept in storage too long! This was a thoroughly debased individual, yet all power resided in his person—how could the Ming Dynasty not collapse, after this? Although he was succeeded after his death, he had already caused the destruction of the Ming. Kangxi was the exact opposite, releasing the energy of his life from the palace out into the wilderness, the hunting fields, and various schools of knowledge. And the Mountain Resort at Chengde was an important breathing space for him."]},{"label":"III","paragraphs":["KANGXI’S DIFFERENCE FROM WANLI, the Chengde Resort versus Wanli’s palace retreat—these comparisons were keenly felt by ethnic Han intellectuals, leading to complicated emotions.","To start with, a majority of the Han intelligentsia were in favor of overthrowing the Qing and restoring the Ming, and long after the valiant military men had retreated, the frail scholars were still clinging firmly to this idea. Although there were a few well-known literary figures who later recanted, even they suffered deep psychological conflict and spiritual torment.","I believe this is the power of culture. Military affairs can only be on the surface, but it is only when a cultural shift is underway that matters become serious.","A people, a country, a race—in the end, these are defined not by armies, or territory, or politics, but by culture. Many of the biggest anti-Qing rebellions in the Jiangnan district were the result of “head-shaving” incidents. For the Han, who’d traditionally kept their hair long, having it forcibly cut off by the Manchus was such an affront that it led to stand-offs in which they were forced to choose between keeping their hair or keeping their heads. Hairstyles might not seem like a particularly significant subject, but they have to do with cultural existence. And so, a question of dress became one of the flashpoints in the clash between Han and non-Han.","In the middle of this, the people best-equipped to place matters in their cultural context are cultural figures themselves, who understand the difference between civilization and barbarism, and it was also these who connected the Manchu “Tartars” with uncivilized behavior. The peasants finally had their hair shaved off, while quite a few scholars resisted fiercely. The famous academic Liu Zongzhou went on a hunger strike after Qing forces entered his home city of Hangzhou, and died twenty days later. His pupil Huang Zongxi, another well-known scholar, threw himself into armed resistance against the Qing; when he was defeated, he returned to his hometown to take care of his mother and focus on his writing. Yet another well-known scholar, Gu Yanwu, began wandering the country after his own attempts at armed resistance against the Qing failed. No one could ever track him down, and he ended up spending his old age in Shaanxi. All these great scholars were strong and full of integrity; many of their students and followers naturally took them as role models.","When it came to Kangxi’s reign, though, there were some subtle shifts. Writers had, like the bald eagle depicted by Zhu Da, a clear-eyed view of “heaven and earth united by frost”—glaring piercingly at the court, while the court seemed strangely unable to hold back its passion for Han culture. At first everyone assumed this was a tactic to win hearts and minds, but no one observing Kangxi’s conduct could believe this was completely the case.","Even in the midst of sending troops to deal with Wu Sangui, he was already hurrying to send out orders to his ministers that, with the objective of “revering Confucianism and revering Taoism,” he wanted them to recommend scholars of “excellent all-round knowledge and ability with words” whom he would personally examine, in order to nominate an especially learned elite.","In total, 143 individuals were nominated and summoned, of which ultimately five were chosen. These included people such as Fu Shan and Li Yong, who refused to respond to the call. Fu Shan had to be forcibly brought to Beijing, where he saw the three-character “Great Qing Gate” and flung himself to the ground, tears streaming from his eyes. Not only did Kangxi not punish him for this behavior, but actually allowed him to bypass the exam, conferring directly upon him the title of “Gentleman of Chinese Letters.” Fu Shan refused to let anyone address him by this name after he’d returned home, but it’s difficult to maintain that he still held as great a grudge against Kangxi himself at this point.","Li Yong, similarly, refused to take the exam—by feigning illness. After being hauled to the capital, he went on a hunger strike in protest, and they had to let him go. This happened in the seventeenth year of Kangxi’s reign, when Kangxi was only twenty-six years old. Twenty-five years after this, the now-fifty-something Kangxi went on a journey to survey the West, and actually remembered this stubborn scholar, summoning him to an interview. Li Yong refused to go, but must have felt some awkwardness about this, because he sent his son Li Shenyan instead to represent him, and to present Kangxi with two of his books: Records of Reflections on the Four Great Texts and Collected Erqu. This had a symbolic meaning, showing that even the most resistant Han intellectuals were beginning to thaw toward Kangxi.","Compared to Li Yong, Huang Zongxi was a much more important figure, and Kangxi treated him with even more ceremony, frequently inviting him to meet, only to be rebuffed. Finally, he sent local patrols to Huang Zongxi’s home, ordering them to copy out all his books, with the copies being sent back to the palace for his perusal. This must have left Huang Zongxi feeling, at the least, a little moved. Like Li Yong, he claimed to be unable to make a personal appearance, and sent his son in his place. Huang Baijia ended up working for the Emperor, compiling historical records, helping Kangxi complete his grand project of creating a History of the Ming. So we see how even someone strongly against the Qing was willing to allow his son to spend a lifetime under the command of Kangxi. This wasn’t recantation or compromise, but merely the beginning of a shift in cultural identity. If Kangxi was so sincere in his recognition of Han culture, could the Han literati really claim to have no point of identification with him?","Didn’t Huang Zongxi allow his son to take part in Kangxi’s compilation of Ming history? The creation of this volume caused quite a stir in the field of Han knowledge. Kangxi gave this responsibility over to the great historians Xu Yuanwen, Wan Sitong, Zhang Yushu and Wang Hongxu, commanding them to be guided by A True Record of the Ming, saying, “Other books may be valued for the beauty of their words, but histories alone must be judged by their facts.” He also frequently reminded his people to pay close attention to the decline and defeat of the Ming Dynasty, and to take note of the lessons that could be learned. The Han intelligentsia was still focused on overthrowing the Qing and restoring the Ming, while the Qing court was itself leading Han historians in an objective study of the Ming Dynasty. This level of analysis was of a higher order than the simple call for revolution, and it was hard for some of this opposition not to melt away. Later on, History of the Ming would become one of the better books in the Twenty-Four Histories, a fact that we must acknowledge even today.","Of course, there remained several writers who steadfastly refused to give in. For instance, there was a scholar in Zhejiang during Kangxi’s reign called Lü Liuliang, who argued in both his writing and teaching that the spirit of Confucian studies was to “honor the ruler and repel the barbarians.” Some time after he died, a scholar in Hunan named Zeng Jing, who’d failed the Imperial Examination, read this formulation and was so moved that he rushed to Zhejiang to find Lü Liuliang’s son and pupils, trying to induce them to join in his struggle against the Qing.","By this point, Kangxi had died and been succeded by Yongzheng. These academic folk had not one soldier under their command—what could they hope to accomplish? Then they heard that the Governor of Chuanshan, Yue Zhongqi, was a descendant of renowned General Yue Fei, and decided he would surely want to fight the barbarians as keenly as his famous forebear. They sent him a letter asking him to join them—openly inciting him to defect.","This sounds like a joke. A lot of time had passed since Yue Fei stood against the Jin—the whole of the Yuan and Ming Dynasties, as well as 89 years of the Qing—and he was many, many generations removed from Yue Zhongqi. The fact that the surname “Yue” alone was enough for an invitation to this conspiracy shows the foolishness and naïveté of the Chinese literati.","Yue Zhongqi was a Qing official, and wouldn’t have imagined getting such a letter in his wildest dreams. He fobbed the conspirators off with a vague reply, but naturally enough reported the matter to Emperor Yongzheng at once. The Emperor gave orders for the scholars to be arrested, then sat down to read the letter and their texts himself. He ended up feeling that they had made several good points, and he needed to write an essay of his own in order to explain himself to the Han intellectuals. Surely Kangxi’s reign was proof enough that the Qing conquerors were not all bad? Why would anyone still want to overthrow the regime? And so Yongzheng published his Record of Great Righteousness Enlightening the Misguided, and distributed it across the country. He also took care to pardon the troublemaker Zeng Jing and his gang, allowing them to return to Zhejiang and give their testimony.","This essay of Yongzheng’s was fairly earnest in its approach. The gist was: Yes, we are barbarians, we are foreigners, but this is an artificial distinction. Heaven has sent us here to nurture the folk of the Central Plains, and why should you Central Plainsmen insist of maintaining this division between “Chinese” and “barbarians?” Your respected King Shun was an eastern barbarian, King Wen of Zhou was a western barbarian—have they harmed your virtue? Even the eminent writer Lü Liuliang felt the need to denigrate and deny the cultural achievement and virtues of the previous monarch Kangxi—this showed he cared more for his petty personal grudge than the good of the nation. You might be angry that people of another tribe now control the Central Plains, but perhaps their courage should be seen as virtue? If scholars paid attention only to a ruler’s birthplace and not to his deeds, if a foreign-born ruler gained no approval no matter how he caused the country to prosper, then even the most generous-hearted outsider monarch would lose momentum, and if that were to happen, wouldn’t it be the peasants of the Central Regions who’d suffer the most?","Yongzheng’s words definitely show that he felt wronged, and that he wanted to reclaim the reputation of his father Kangxi. This essay is undoubtedly moving in places, although taken as a whole, it shows that this Emperor was not the intellectual equal of his father. By repeatedly referring to himself as a “foreigner” and “barbarian,” he played into preconceived notions, creating confusion. His son Qianlong would later realize this. He recalled all copies of Record of Great Righteousness Enlightening the Misguided and banned it, executed Zeng Jing and the others who’d been spared by Yongzheng, and touched off another round of the literary inquisition.","Apart from his sensitivity around the Han versus barbarian issue, Yongzheng was in many ways a tolerant, measured monarch. He was able to accept the sharp criticisms and suggestions of loyal ministers and other men of merit, and thus accomplished a great many good things during his early years in power. You could argue that the country prospered under him. In this scenario, the remaining dissenting Han intellectuals wouldn’t dare get any ideas, and by the end their opposing viewpoints were totally gone. Not that there were many such individuals in the first place, given that Yongzheng and Qianlong had already put out their manifestos. The truly first-rate scholars had given up the idea of opposing the Qing during Qianlong’s reign, anyway.","Qianlong relied on the vast number of talented individuals under his command, on the solid foundation left behind by Kangxi and Yongzheng, and on his own abilities as a military strategist—and of all China’s Emperors, his reign saw the most good fortune. He paid the most visits to Chengde Resort, and spent much more time there—therefore the place shows the traces of his presence the most. Qianlong also frequented Mulan Hunting Reserve, and shot an impressive number of animals, but his heart and mind were fixated on the border wars. Battle slogans began proliferating on stone tablets in the villa grounds and the eight temples surrounding it.","Such warmongering was not incompatible with the wishes of the Han people, and in fact raised China’s status, some of the glory even accruing to the Han intellectuals, a group of whom began to see Qianlong as the sage of Cathay. After I’d studied the inscriptions on the stone tablets closely, though, I felt a question percolating strongly through me: were such battles really necessary? So many of them? And did the killings need to be so cruel?","Rejoicing in his grandiose accomplishments, Qianlong carved evidence of his so-called “Ten Great Campaigns” all over Chengde, which filled the place with an atmosphere of over-heated ill fortune. Long after the Manchu and Han opposition had faded away, this villa would reek of the Chinese Empire’s smugness.","On 14th September 1793, a British diplomatic mission came to the villa, and Qianlong welcomed them with a great banquet, even holding a song-and-dance performance and torch-lit evening gala in the tree-filled groves, so all was gaiety and celebration. The British had come with the objective of seeking permission to dispatch an ambassador to Beijing, giving Western businesses an anchor there, so China would open its borders to free trade, even designating some districts near Guangzhou as residences for British merchants. They also hoped for tax breaks on British goods shipped along the rivers connecting Guangzhou and Macao. This should have been a matter for discussion, but to Qianlong, secure in his mountain resort and fond of showing off his dazzling military might on behalf of Cathay, there was no possibility of negotiation.","He wrote a letter to George III with the title “A Decree From The Emperor to the English King,” completely rejecting every single request from the other side, declaring, “Every inch of the territory of our Empire is marked on the map and the strictest vigilance is exercised over it all: even tiny islets and outlying sandbanks are clearly defined as part of the provinces to which they belong…the subjects of our dependencies have never been allowed to open places of business in Peking…the demands presented by your embassy are not only a contravention of dynastic tradition, but would be utterly unproductive of good benefits to yourself, besides being quite impracticable.” Even today, people regard these words as full of patriotic fervor and stern righteousness, not to be spoken of in the same breath as the traitorous treaties signed by the later Qing. I can’t agree with this sentiment.","In 1684, Kangxi had already lifted the ban on overseas trade, establishing customs ports at Guangdong, Fujian, Zhejiang and Jiangsu. After more than seventy years, Qianlong had closed three of these down, only permitting trade at Guangdong, and even then instituting many laughable restrictions, for instance forbidding merchants to learn Chinese, buy Chinese books or ride sedan chairs, and particularly not to bring women ashore. It’s easy to imagine what these restrictions said about Qing Dynasty attitudes to people from other countries.","Kangxi learned the Western natural sciences from missionaries and had a good relationship with them, whereas Qianlong banned Catholicism from China.","Qianlong’s scolding words, sent to a foreign monarch from his mountain villa, seem unbecoming in a historical figure. Ill omens seemed to be seeping into this garden."]},{"label":"IV","paragraphs":["ON THE WESTERN SIDE of a tablet in the Qianlong Temple at Songyun Gorge, within the villa grounds, I read a few lines composed by his son Jiaqing. This was after Jiaqing had ascended the throne—passing by this place, he saw the self-satisfied poems composed by his father, and couldn’t help sighing that they seemed so deep, whereas he, the son, felt the burdens of kingship keenly. (“Gazing upon the accumulated depths, my position rests heavily on my shoulders.”)","Jiaqing’s entire reign was filled with troubles from inside and outside his borders, and in the end he died in the summer resort of unknown causes.","The Daoguang Emperor was already approaching forty when he succeeded Jiaqing, and appeared to have no particular aptitude for it. All he knew was suffering and frugality, insisting on patching his trousers when they were torn rather than getting new ones. This is not praiseworthy in the leader of a country. His ministers actually tried to imitate him, wearing patched old clothes to court, leading to a decline in dignity all round.","As his father died at Chengde, timorous Daoguang refused to set foot there, and so it was locked up for several decades. From time to time, he’d consider going hunting as his ancestors had, only to shelve these plans when he discovered that there was no way to get to Mulan Hunting Reserve without passing by the mountain villa. Such a pitiable figure would seem to have little destiny, in any case, connecting him to Chengde or to hunting. When the Opium War broke out, he could only fix his worried gaze southward.","Chengde Resort was shuttered until September 1860. Suddenly, word arrived that the Xianfeng Emperor was on his way, and the place should be quickly cleaned up. Xianfeng arrived with a large quantity of silver. It turned out he was fleeing for his life, as the British and French allied army was threatening Beijing. He would never leave these grounds again, spending the rest of his reign wandering through the gardens, grateful his ancestors had left him such a good hiding place. From here, he signed several treaties that were massively injurious to China’s rights and dignity—and even then refused to budge. He died on August 22, 1861, about a year after he’d arrived at the villa.","Xianfeng’s death caused another flurry of activity at Chengde. Several political factions circled around the body, carrying out a series of shady negotiations. A scheme that later came to be known as the “Xinyou Coup” was hammered out in the various outbuildings on the grounds. As Xianfeng’s bier was borne towards Beijing, his young successor also strode boldly forth, and the gates of Chengde were once again tightly locked. A twenty-seven-year-old woman quickly gained prominence in the ensuing scrum. She was destined to govern China for several decades.","This was the Empress Dowager Cixi, and she would never return to Chengde. Not long after leaving, she issued an order: Rihe Palace had fallen out of use for several years, and several of its pavilions and buildings were in danger of collapse; the Xianfeng Emperor had only returned to recuperate for a time, and now that he had passed away and his entourage departed, “all construction work on Rihe will stop with immediate effect.”","This statement echoed Kangxi’s earlier decision not to restore the Great Wall. The wall had since fallen into total disrepair, overgrown with weeds, crows flapping overhead, the old structures crumbling and covered in lichen, while the gates remained firmly shut.","Boarding up the palace buildings wouldn’t matter much, but to lock away those deep green hills, those bright waters—Kangxi saw this place as the Qing Dynasty of his heart, yet the Dynasty had now seen fit to abandon it. The pitiful Chengde Villa, and the even more pitiful Qing Dynasty, now lacked even a round-backed chair to sit upon, lonely and desolate, soulless.","Later on, Cixi restored the Summer Palace in Beijing, intending it to “confront” the Chengde villa. The gardens to the north of the wall no longer had the ability or energy to “confront” anything, already seeming to belong to another era. The lofty air of Rihe had long since blown away, and the Qing Dynasty would from now on be full of dark energy, riven with faultlines.","As a new century dawned, a group of Han intellectuals began issuing destructive criticisms of the regime. By now, Chengde had turned into a negative symbol, hiding in the distance and hoping not to be noticed."]},{"label":"V","paragraphs":["THE QING DYNASTY FELL, turning society upside down, sowing chaos across the country. Not until the great scholar Wang Guowei drowned himself in the Summer palace on June 2, 1927, did those who still had their wits about them pause for sober reflection.","Much has been said about Mr. Wang Guowei’s reasons for dying, and we don’t need to go into them. All we need to know is that this master of Han culture, bearing the braided queue of the times, ended his life in the grounds of the Qing imperial gardens. He left behind a final message: “I’ve lived more than fifty years, and have nothing more to do but die. Living through such transformations of the world, I fear no further humiliation.”","He must have known that as the Ming waned and the Qing rose, Han people had lost their lives in the fight over whether they could keep their hair long and in buns, instead of being forced to pull it into queues. He must also have been aware of the generous actions of Liu Zongzhou, Huang Zongxi, and Gu Yanwu, and even more so of the progress of world history, and the enormous changes to society that took place as a matter of course. And yet, he died all the same.","I agree with Mr. Chen Yinke when he writes that Mr. Wang didn’t die of a political struggle or personal quarrel, but because of a certain type of culture:","Whenever a particular cultural value declines, those who have changed themselves for it will naturally undergo torment—the weightier the culture, the greater the torment. In extreme cases, suicide might become the only method by which solace can be found.","—“Preface to a Tribute to Mr. Wang Guantang”","Mr. Wang Guowei was truly unable to separate his culture from the Qing Dynasty. On his bookshelves were The Imperial Encyclopedia, The Dictionary of Kangxi, The Complete Library in Four Sections, A Dream of the Red Chamber, Peach Blossom Fan, The Palace of Eternal Life, Qianlong and Jiaqing studies, and the works of the Manchu poet Nalan Xingde. All these words before him, and he cleaved to every book, every page. As far as he could see, all that surrounded him had been destroyed—not just a political ideal, but an entire culture, a classical era.","All he wanted was to hold on to ancient times.","Let’s not forget that under Kangxi, the most important Han intellectuals began to create connections to the Qing court after undergoing fierce struggles with themselves. Who could have imagined that after Kangxi’s enterprise failed, these cultural ties would also be severed? As a result, the extremely learned Mr. Wang Guowei gave his life to mark this loss, rather than struggling anew with the desires of his heart to find a new hope—a pitiful, yet inevitable, death.","The intelligentsia will always deviate from the norm. After political or military upheavals, they will always display a long-lasting cultural resilience. This becomes their lives, and they have no other way forward but to wrap their entire existence around culture. So things went at the end of the Ming Dynasty, so again they went at the end of the Qing.","The great fragility and enormous strength of culture is amply displayed in the splash of Mr. Wang Guowei’s body hitting the water.","This was Mr. Wang’s first visit to the summer palace. He borrowed five yuan from a colleague before heading in. The entrance fee was sixty cents, and after his death, four yuan and forty cents was found in his pocket. He couldn’t go to Chengde, and wouldn’t have been able to push open the tightly locked gates of the mountain villa.","As I stood facing the clear lake waters of the resort, I couldn’t help thinking of Wang Guowei’s face and silhouette, and sighed lightly: a dynasty lasting many centuries always starts with a heroic band of strong individuals, but when the final period is dotted on the last sentence, all that is left is usually some refined, mournful souls."]}]}};
